1eedfb75a65037fca3403ebebfd96"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-117800" :question/id #uuid "c44f6521-15eb-5c8e-93a8-cab320c1122a" :question/math-academy-id "q-117800" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/6003a85e0038b7e6562381cd338a7597877d1152adbeb476b7141eee74ba06ee.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}-0=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{2\\pi }{5})}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $\\frac{5}{4}$ units up. Hence, $D=\\frac{5}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{5}{4}=\\frac{15}{4}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2})+\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-117800/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dac9c72b-c23f-56fa-b939-6e3b56d3a13b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-117800"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-65550/selection/7627fb02fec1c7a865cb3e7797f7ea7835cb8ec3423f266885f8f5d01084e358" :answer/id #uuid "b1c2c1eb-db74-5e2c-bd88-9f3da1804b2f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65550/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "31136edc-d135-5bc5-9b5d-0b937c221afe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65550/selection/c0c9fe5b5e67591ea1a9a278e613c7db3f4b1066f6b217a6dd18b8063d756111" :answer/id #uuid "d3ccaef8-8575-5851-a29a-8efa68f8a05e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{17}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65550/selection/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" :answer/id #uuid "1f62e4b8-261e-5898-a374-81526ea71b04" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65550/selection/b01ba7e917b2f2dc3ff90ccb2c435764c9ebef2dea263cc318e0c231eb7eeb1d" :answer/id #uuid "b49b83fd-1776-57d2-abe2-14ebdae03ada" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65550/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7d443759-daaa-55fa-bfa3-463af17a09d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-65550/selection/c0c9fe5b5e67591ea1a9a278e613c7db3f4b1066f6b217a6dd18b8063d756111" :answer-field/choices ["q-65550/selection/7627fb02fec1c7a865cb3e7797f7ea7835cb8ec3423f266885f8f5d01084e358" "q-65550/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-65550/selection/c0c9fe5b5e67591ea1a9a278e613c7db3f4b1066f6b217a6dd18b8063d756111" "q-65550/selection/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" "q-65550/selection/b01ba7e917b2f2dc3ff90ccb2c435764c9ebef2dea263cc318e0c231eb7eeb1d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-65550" :question/id #uuid "2a31794b-90ed-5858-8a23-bda34267af75" :question/math-academy-id "q-65550" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/c904a1eb628e726647a6f655a567a0939f7b7d404f0b32c6c4b4adaeca847916.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B-D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{4\\pi }{5}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{4\\pi }{5})}=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $3$ units down. Hence, $D=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $B-D=(\\frac{5}{4})-(-3)=\\frac{5}{4}+3=\\frac{17}{4}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{5x}{4})-3.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-65550/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dac9c72b-c23f-56fa-b939-6e3b56d3a13b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-65550"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-65549"] :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{5}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{\\pi }{5})}=5.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $\\frac{3}{2}$ units up. Hence, $D=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $B-2D=5-2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})=5-3=2.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5x)+\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-79188"] :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}=3.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $3$ units up. Hence, $D=3.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=3+3=6.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)+3.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-143665/selection/843cac81a39ec420eeda5b659ca0c95fca768adb954c51387f58e06c289a039c" :answer/id #uuid "eec6d319-df77-5fee-a246-a68e8ccc6599" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{17}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143665/selection/8e918182341451051ce2dca07cfe2b17fa3abe4a925a8df02308f239a4dd798f" :answer/id #uuid "04b8ff0c-3594-5f2b-9085-89943bf5318e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143665/selection/671723d87f1a67d9aa6a1f954b934e271d81eedfb75a65037fca3403ebebfd96" :answer/id #uuid "5cd468a0-9b78-530a-a9b3-4d5d92a61124" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{13}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143665/selection/c3e6f546a187bdf3e90f22c43e845035e89694013a54be09f0aa8bccb24fc7bc" :answer/id #uuid "f6e73a94-f30c-5985-93e9-e5e8fece272d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{23}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143665/selection/b8fe8d3cb42910de21f3df8634e1c373223a74d32bfef959cca9ceacd5c4d03e" :answer/id #uuid "f082efb1-4250-5c5e-b202-93cbc4bd2c1b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143665/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9d05ad20-f842-564d-9b39-68acf66fba8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-143665/selection/b8fe8d3cb42910de21f3df8634e1c373223a74d32bfef959cca9ceacd5c4d03e" :answer-field/choices ["q-143665/selection/843cac81a39ec420eeda5b659ca0c95fca768adb954c51387f58e06c289a039c" "q-143665/selection/8e918182341451051ce2dca07cfe2b17fa3abe4a925a8df02308f239a4dd798f" "q-143665/selection/671723d87f1a67d9aa6a1f954b934e271d81eedfb75a65037fca3403ebebfd96" "q-143665/selection/c3e6f546a187bdf3e90f22c43e845035e89694013a54be09f0aa8bccb24fc7bc" "q-143665/selection/b8fe8d3cb42910de21f3df8634e1c373223a74d32bfef959cca9ceacd5c4d03e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-143665" :question/id #uuid "ea1ca9e7-5ad3-5b9e-a96e-f5ccff3aca09" :question/math-academy-id "q-143665" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/39d3bd73ac0f60f21cc9e71cbf46d62901fcc07cab3b06cd84a496b1e1415a1a.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $5$ units down. Hence, $D=-5.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{3}{2}+(-5)=-\\frac{7}{2}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2})-5.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-143665/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dac9c72b-c23f-56fa-b939-6e3b56d3a13b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-143665"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-79195/selection/e0bd991ac2e2f2a23ec920e03c6d5103be6b20c12a25d724ab290d4ba5292c3e" :answer/id #uuid "31d82c98-47ea-5443-b60d-d110accf3073" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79195/selection/adbad78d33491a071139fe589fefda7e9ba65cf97ebbcae14d252ac90459e198" :answer/id #uuid "a91c85b7-87bb-59ab-8ea8-5c95f155f09f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79195/selection/42d84e4a0457035d14dfcae2406dc9405241c35bc79e6c69f43f243d88fd8bc7" :answer/id #uuid "0a342a72-b5db-52a8-bbbc-4f6c459093e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79195/selection/a14f8f42753c29ee5989171c1d52e7e8ac41d6176b4b32daf171ad5a7853d459" :answer/id #uuid "3ae513fa-fd63-570f-961b-db18f9c86ddc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{-\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79195/selection/82f0679237d603c9b88d77fbc6f240065a24a3716631fab4d9f58426a7f011e5" :answer/id #uuid "c2abc93f-c5ef-5767-9f98-f5421b4b7328" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79195/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "36837e76-b2da-51d2-a076-35871200dfc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-79195/selection/82f0679237d603c9b88d77fbc6f240065a24a3716631fab4d9f58426a7f011e5" :answer-field/choices ["q-79195/selection/e0bd991ac2e2f2a23ec920e03c6d5103be6b20c12a25d724ab290d4ba5292c3e" "q-79195/selection/adbad78d33491a071139fe589fefda7e9ba65cf97ebbcae14d252ac90459e198" "q-79195/selection/42d84e4a0457035d14dfcae2406dc9405241c35bc79e6c69f43f243d88fd8bc7" "q-79195/selection/a14f8f42753c29ee5989171c1d52e7e8ac41d6176b4b32daf171ad5a7853d459" "q-79195/selection/82f0679237d603c9b88d77fbc6f240065a24a3716631fab4d9f58426a7f011e5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-79195" :question/id #uuid "adbc630c-edd8-52e4-9bd9-f612c8f44da5" :question/math-academy-id "q-79195" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/89efc20f1c6527d1bcd501f97ce585fb5a60e9791804f56d61f33653cf1a70d7.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{3}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/f8617da98ea9d9fb48b8fef588b624c6961a299233836fe433c1819b697ca0fb.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)$ by $0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{12})=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ units to the left. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-79195/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84355770-5926-5be6-9b9f-c13bb05599ae"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-79195"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-143677/selection/adbad78d33491a071139fe589fefda7e9ba65cf97ebbcae14d252ac90459e198" :answer/id #uuid "c32bdca9-cd10-5d08-bf2a-49619ae4741c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143677/selection/9d08cdaaebcb50b75e0c4899a76bc48fdcf67c6a2cb8ba53dbaede2dc5354629" :answer/id #uuid "c3d5962b-c7d1-5864-858d-fe3f1d39738a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143677/selection/e0bd991ac2e2f2a23ec920e03c6d5103be6b20c12a25d724ab290d4ba5292c3e" :answer/id #uuid "1111f967-7f83-575f-ac96-99557c1be78b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143677/selection/a3f72a33bd97dbcb26e67e320a072cf6fd98f5dc94f2de44db2fa04c176254bd" :answer/id #uuid "5babeeef-a8f1-511a-b58f-bed13852c167" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143677/selection/1e940709a1b00fa910544fd0093551b452ea4b241ad5d9959525312e8a27fa19" :answer/id #uuid "6b673c6a-01e8-5d9e-8f0f-74d7c698a37c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143677/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a13a5095-aa8b-5e41-9483-27f8aedf50a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-143677/selection/e0bd991ac2e2f2a23ec920e03c6d5103be6b20c12a25d724ab290d4ba5292c3e" :answer-field/choices ["q-143677/selection/adbad78d33491a071139fe589fefda7e9ba65cf97ebbcae14d252ac90459e198" "q-143677/selection/9d08cdaaebcb50b75e0c4899a76bc48fdcf67c6a2cb8ba53dbaede2dc5354629" "q-143677/selection/e0bd991ac2e2f2a23ec920e03c6d5103be6b20c12a25d724ab290d4ba5292c3e" "q-143677/selection/a3f72a33bd97dbcb26e67e320a072cf6fd98f5dc94f2de44db2fa04c176254bd" "q-143677/selection/1e940709a1b00fa910544fd0093551b452ea4b241ad5d9959525312e8a27fa19"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-143677" :question/id #uuid "3090bea4-a609-5a53-a6eb-a8060c8d0e8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-143677" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/7602d1695610361f28613d8bf3542056b2624f9b6b8980873f6fdebf802c6750.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3}+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}(x+\\frac{3C}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{3C}{2}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}(x+\\frac{3C}{2}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3}).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/015ca9336646a7545de842414ace2c2a695688e30653b2ed019000d69609d56d.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3})$ by $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ units to the left. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3C}{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,⟹\\,C=(\\frac{\\pi }{4})(\\frac{2}{3})\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-143677/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84355770-5926-5be6-9b9f-c13bb05599ae"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-143677"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-143675/selection/1799e19c9a3a0d0b1889be2c54ab73f5730109da85155b3b8b0ccf6c4adbeaca" :answer/id #uuid "2469eb49-f945-51a6-bd67-385181ca9534" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143675/selection/4f4832c36defb1ed86287b2874a58927849dd3eb93f2306b966c53090e40ee49" :answer/id #uuid "b856d1ba-4cd2-57e0-9ec0-38c7ecf1ab64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\pi }{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143675/selection/18d2529b02c578c82d2a615fc876d3bce68cbc096c16c0fe2f664ec05c3c71f9" :answer/id #uuid "88175730-87a0-57dc-b329-d16999ac8f97" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143675/selection/d62732e72e17b0393290bd9e548ef368f6b7424bded817d038b07db5bf5fcb98" :answer/id #uuid "a9b6ab24-a082-5ef0-9200-3272b8d65bf7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143675/selection/a3f72a33bd97dbcb26e67e320a072cf6fd98f5dc94f2de44db2fa04c176254bd" :answer/id #uuid "a2c2adbd-5475-5287-90ba-9d5e3581e2dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143675/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7062cce4-e6fb-5055-884a-402f9e6d7825" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-143675/selection/4f4832c36defb1ed86287b2874a58927849dd3eb93f2306b966c53090e40ee49" :answer-field/choices ["q-143675/selection/1799e19c9a3a0d0b1889be2c54ab73f5730109da85155b3b8b0ccf6c4adbeaca" "q-143675/selection/4f4832c36defb1ed86287b2874a58927849dd3eb93f2306b966c53090e40ee49" "q-143675/selection/18d2529b02c578c82d2a615fc876d3bce68cbc096c16c0fe2f664ec05c3c71f9" "q-143675/selection/d62732e72e17b0393290bd9e548ef368f6b7424bded817d038b07db5bf5fcb98" "q-143675/selection/a3f72a33bd97dbcb26e67e320a072cf6fd98f5dc94f2de44db2fa04c176254bd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-143675" :question/id #uuid "ed308f35-6936-5e15-9ba0-feff8b2097a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-143675" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/a8fbbaff1139a0c80c5dc97a9d99a08e238a61ab65251a158b382e208be7e842.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4(x+\\frac{C}{4}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{4}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4(x+\\frac{C}{4}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/4c5147d95cc4d5df1d847f5fe9f580d9595d0f4d8c2c8209efa1f2a3cd9c5aea.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x)$ by $\\frac{9\\pi }{40}-\\frac{\\pi }{8}=\\frac{\\pi }{10}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{4}=-\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-143675/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84355770-5926-5be6-9b9f-c13bb05599ae"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-143675"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-143670/selection/82f0679237d603c9b88d77fbc6f240065a24a3716631fab4d9f58426a7f011e5" :answer/id #uuid "d4951cd9-0945-56bd-a84a-946ff91b45e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143670/selection/adbad78d33491a071139fe589fefda7e9ba65cf97ebbcae14d252ac90459e198" :answer/id #uuid "6117de41-0aa9-518b-9753-471cb40e4c7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143670/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "5a878c8d-02ea-5839-90c7-fc1ac2afaa26" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143670/selection/3826cb6859afc17fc29beb5847fa2ea888053c16a97c5e0ca18d51a86cfb06ca" :answer/id #uuid "a9f3b74f-4ebc-55f9-95d6-162cfe8df179" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143670/selection/09e535b811e1e7584404784c7ac4f577a3ed9805710046fc8fb895ffb3de13a0" :answer/id #uuid "0cf71456-e433-5f2e-a7e9-9c96c8a84c3c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143670/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "bd574ae3-6c39-5abd-92e9-c19a6010730b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-143670/selection/09e535b811e1e7584404784c7ac4f577a3ed9805710046fc8fb895ffb3de13a0" :answer-field/choices ["q-143670/selection/82f0679237d603c9b88d77fbc6f240065a24a3716631fab4d9f58426a7f011e5" "q-143670/selection/adbad78d33491a071139fe589fefda7e9ba65cf97ebbcae14d252ac90459e198" "q-143670/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-143670/selection/3826cb6859afc17fc29beb5847fa2ea888053c16a97c5e0ca18d51a86cfb06ca" "q-143670/selection/09e535b811e1e7584404784c7ac4f577a3ed9805710046fc8fb895ffb3de13a0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-143670" :question/id #uuid "af1bf5b7-d6f3-5371-8c7d-a519f70d9653" :question/math-academy-id "q-143670" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/c2e5b01cf03f1f9d82562ee51fc484448e90b74dd2584daf18a499849ca36bff.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}(x+3C))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+3C$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}(x+3C))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/29346a073d6a22f0bab74eeac86431e8c761c9ed21a005ec6965f71e6436c117.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})$ by $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n3C=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{12}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $-\\frac{\\pi }{12}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-143670/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84355770-5926-5be6-9b9f-c13bb05599ae"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-143670"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-65570"] :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}(x+2C))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+2C$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}(x+2C))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=tan\\,(\\frac{x}{2}).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/8a19beac86998c845d7ffa225a52d425ab48b9ec350f6c8fe42f7c6dbce027ce.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ units to the left. Therefore:\n\n$$\n2C=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{8}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
